## Core Principle

**Complete and consistent changes only. Partial or inconsistent work is failure, not progress.**

Be concise in all interactions and commit messages. Sacrifice grammar for brevity.
End each plan with a list of unresolved questions (if any). Keep questions brief.

## Challenge Weak Requirements; Count Supporting Machinery

A feature's cost is itself PLUS every piece of supporting machinery it drags in (state fields, widened function signatures, generic UI components, config/API surface). A human hand-writing that plumbing feels friction that acts as a design signal — AI doesn't, so replace it with explicit habits:

- When a request is weakly specified or weakly motivated, question the requirement FIRST (what need? who uses it? does an existing mechanism serve it better?) instead of implementing it well.
- When proposing a feature, enumerate its supporting machinery as part of the price. Machinery only one feature needs is a red flag — prefer deriving from existing state.
- Before extending a mechanism, cheaply check whether it's used at all (grep / DB query). Prefer removing unused mechanics over building on top.

Example failure: a vague "reward fast answers" idea (`speed_bonus_values`) dragged in an order-tracking state field on two structs, a widened scoring signature, a generic UI component, and config/API surface — never used by any stored design; removal touched 12+ files.

## Always Use Generators

When a framework generator exists for an artifact (Rails migrations/models/controllers, `cargo new`, `npm create`, etc.), run the generator first and rework its output with Edit. Never hand-write files to skip the generator's startup time — generators carry the framework's current conventions (canonical timestamps, naming, placement, base classes); hand-written files carry invented ones. Example failure: hand-rounded Rails migration timestamps caused a silent master/feature version collision.

## User Habits

Vim user — reflexively hits Esc as a "reset to normal mode" gesture with no intent to cancel. Esc is unbound from `chat:cancel` in `~/.claude/keybindings.json` (Chat context). Ctrl-C is the interrupt key. Editor mode stays on `normal` (not vim mode), because vim mode makes slash commands trigger search.

**Commits are mine.** Never offer to commit or ask "want me to commit?" — I review the working tree and commit through lazygit. Finish a change by leaving a clean, verified tree, a concise summary of what changed, and the commit message(s) ready to paste; one message per logical commit when the work splits. Commit only when explicitly asked (trunk pattern, straight to `main`). Message format: one-line summary (~60 chars), blank line, 3–6 line body. The body carries only what the diff can't show and the docs don't already record — the one insight that makes the diff make sense, operational consequences ("no data migration needed"), rejected alternatives someone might "fix" back. No file lists, no UI enumeration, no rationale already in code comments or CLAUDE.md. Never a giant single-line message: the commit overview shows first lines only.

**Intent beats mechanical rules.** A lint/format rule is a proxy for readability; when the proxy and the goal conflict, the goal wins. Before enabling a style check, ask whether it can tell an intentional idiom from a mistake — if it can't (Credo `SinglePipe` judging "prepare `|>` execute" by arity), keep it as a documented convention plus review instead of machine enforcement, and say explicitly in the docs which rules are enforced and which are conventions. When a formatter or linter blocks a deliberate readability choice, surface the tradeoff rather than silently complying.

## Claude Code Harness

A `Bash` call with `run_in_background: true` lives in the runtime's task list (TaskList/TaskStop id like `b2k34a8ns`), not as a job of any later shell — `kill %1` from a later Bash call sees no jobs and does nothing. The runtime counts itself "still working" while any background task is alive, so an orphan shows as endless processing dots and prompts that have to be force-sent. Before spawning one, prefer `Monitor` or a one-shot foreground `until … done`; after spawning one, keep the returned task id and `TaskStop` it when done. On stuck dots or forced prompts, check for live tasks first.

## Secrets: 1Password `agents` vault via op-agent

Never read key files, paste secrets, or put secret values in config/env blocks. Every API key and token an agent or a dev shell needs is an item in the `agents` vault (account `agilerbv.1password.com`): one item per service (`mailgun`, `transip`, ...), one field per variable, field named like the variable; project-only secrets go in an item named after the project.

- `op-agent` (`~/.local/bin`, from dotfiles) is `op` already authenticated for this machine. Use it for everything op, never bare `op`.
- Projects keep a committed dotenv of references, no values: `MAILGUN_API_KEY=op://agents/mailgun/MAILGUN_API_KEY`. Run the consumer under it: `op-agent run --env-file .env -- <cmd>`. Only `<cmd>` sees the values; expand variables inside `sh -c '...'`, never in the parent shell. op conceals values in output; never print or decode them.
- Single value straight into a consumer: `op-agent read op://agents/<item>/<field> | <consumer>`, never echoed.
- MCP servers that need a secret (mailgun today): `command` is the absolute path of `op-agent`, args `run --env-file ~/.config/op/<service>.env -- <absolute cmd> ...`; no `env` block, no shell wrapper. `op run` fetches the values when the server starts, and Claude starts every enabled server at session start, so an enabled server costs one 1Password prompt per session whether or not its tools are used. Therefore: never user scope; add it only in the projects that use the service, with `claude mcp add -s local <name> -- <op-agent> run --env-file ... -- <cmd>` run from that project, and leave it disabled there (`/mcp` toggles it; stored per project in `disabledMcpServers` of `~/.claude.json`). Enable it for the session that needs it (mailgun/transip: server provisioning, rarely afterwards). Secret-free servers (http docs servers, tidewave) stay enabled.
- Items and fields are created in the desktop app on a laptop. Ask the user for names; `op-agent item list --vault agents` shows what exists.
- Prod-only secrets (e.g. engage AWS S3 keys) stay as items in other vaults + Fly secrets, never in `agents`; dev reaches prod resources only through the app's API.
- Assume every item points at production (transip, mailgun, engage, schoolbieb, ...): real data, only the changes the user asks for, no exploratory writes. The exception is a field with `DEMO` in its name: a demo account on a production system, free to exercise. Dev credentials are not in 1Password at all; make them or pass them inline, throwaway data and key.
- Items in `agents` (field = variable name): mailgun (`MAILGUN_API_KEY`, `MAILGUN_API_REGION`), transip (`TRANSIP_TOKEN`, `TRANSIP_API_URL`), engage (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `ENGAGE_API_KEY`, `ENGAGE_BASE_URL`; dev server + data_analysis/engage scripts), group_planner (`KLASSENPLANNER_API_URL`, `SPORTTEAMPLANNER_API_URL`, `KLASSENPLANNER_DEMO_API_TOKEN`, `SPORTTEAMPLANNER_DEMO_API_TOKEN`, `KLASSENPLANNER_NVR_API_TOKEN`; prod coordinator tokens, DEMO orgs free to exercise, NVR is the real school: maintain it on request only), schoolbieb (`SCHOOLBIEB_NVR_API_KEY`, `SCHOOLBIEB_NVR_URL`; prod API as agent, same NVR rule). `ser8-agent service account` is the server's own read token, not a service. MCP reference dotenvs live in `~/.config/op/<service>.env`.
{{ if eq .role "server" -}}
- This machine: headless, no 1Password app, no SSH agent. `op-agent` injects the service-account token from `~/.config/op/ser8-agent.token` (0600) into that single process. Never read that file, never export `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN`, never put it in a unit's `Environment=`/`EnvironmentFile=`. The token sees only `agents`; the SSH key vault is unreachable by design, do not work around it.
- systemd user units: `LoadCredential=op-token:%h/.config/op/ser8-agent.token`, then `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN="$(cat $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/op-token)"` inside a `sh -c` ExecStart.
{{- else -}}
